% \section*{Introduction}
%
% This package implements the house style of Ghent University (version 2025)
% for letters, course notes, master/bachelor/PhD dissertations, reports 
% (meeting notes or course assignments), exams, and slides (beamer).
% These class files allow you to make and keep most of your document needs
% compliant to this version and future versions of the UGent house style. 
%
% If you think (1) there's an error in compliancy with regards to the house
% style, (2) there's a feature missing in the classes or beamer theme, or
% (3) there's a bug in this package, please,
% \href{\githubmain/issues}{open an issue on GitHub} or contact me via
% email (\href{mailto:seppe.ongena@ugent.be}{seppe.ongena@ugent.be}).%
%
%
% \section*{Synopsis}
% The |course| and |phd| classes\footnote{For readability the class names
% have been abbreviated by omitting the |ugent| prefix} are an extension
% of the standard \LaTeX{} |book| class. They are intended to be used for
% writing course texts and PhD dissertations and provide a cover page compliant
% to the UGent house style. They also typeset the data pages automatically
% (e.g., jury list, title page, copyright) and style the rest of the document
% appropriately. These two classes additionally enable the generation of 
% a full book cover (back/spine/front) with auto-generated ISBN barcode.
%
% The |bama| and |report| classes are also derived from the standard \LaTeX{}
% |book| class. These are intended for writing BSc/MSc dissertations and
% generic reports (e.g. research reports, course assignments, meeting notes).
% The |bama| class additionally populates a copyright page with signature fields
% based on the authors, supervisors, and tutors added.
%
% The |letter| class is derived from the standard \LaTeX{} |letter| class.
% It is intended to be used for writing business letters compliant to the house
% style and allows for using left-aligned windowed envelopes of the DL format.
%
% The |exam| class is derived from the standard \LaTeX{} |article| class.
% It allows typesetting bilingual exams.
%
% The slides come under the form of a beamer theme. The beamer theme is based
% on the modern 2025 UGent Panno Text one (with support for Arial), \emph{not}
% the 2016 Arial theme as that does not adhere to, e.g., grid spacing and feels
% less refined / more loud in general (large font size of slide titles,
% in both caps and underline).
%
% \begin{center}
%   \framebox[0.95\textwidth]{
%    \begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
%      The documentation of the class files for PhD dissertations, 
%      MSc/BSc dissertations, letters, course texts, reports,
%      and exams can be found in this document (Part~\ref{P:I}).
%
%      Template files for all of the classes can be found below in 
%      Part~I Section~\ref{S:examples}.
%
%      The documentation for the beamer theme is embedded in the
%      demo/user-guide presentation |beamerthemeugentuserguide.pdf|.
%      (Part~\ref{P:II})
%
%    \end{minipage}
%   }
% \end{center}
%
% A number of packages are required by |ugentdocs|. \watchout See Part~I 
% Section~\ref{S:packages} for an overview. Make sure these packages are
% available to your \LaTeX{} compiler. Most of these should be installed by
% default in TeXLive distributions or can be made to auto-install for MikTeX.
%
% \section*{How to read this documentation}
% \begin{itemize}
%	\item When an option or macro (command) is being described, its name
%		 is shown in the left margin.
%	\item If an option is being described instead of a macro,
%		this is shown in the margin by the \textit{(Opt)} note.
%	\item If a macro is mandatory, '(mandatory)' is shown in the left margin.
%		A placeholder is set for that macro, which you should override.
%	\item If a macro sets a tag -- e.g., the |\date| macro sets the |\@date|
%		tag -- it is noted by a '(tag)' text in the left margin. See the tag
%		note further in this list on how to retrieve tags for use.
%	\item The classes for which an option or macro is available are given
%		between square brackets in the margin, e.g. [phd\|course].
%	\item Most options can be set as |option=value| in your
%		\txtcmd{documentclass[<options>]\{class\}} command, but also plain
%		|value| works.
%	\item This: |<name>| is a placeholder. Replace it with your own text.
%	\item For macros, mandatory arguments are shown between curly braces \{\},
%		and optional arguments are shown between square braces [], just as they
%		are when you use them.
%	\item When implementation code is shown, in the docstrip guard blocks
%		denoting for which classes the code is available, some shorthand class
%		names are used, e.g., |crs=course|, |rp=report|, |le=letter|, |ex=exam|,
% 		|bkc=bookcover|, and |bmr=beamer|. |bama| remains |bama|.
%	\item In general, \txtcmd{macros} are commands you can use in your main.tex
%		document file. 
%	\item Some \txtcmd{@macros} or \txtcmd{@tags} are for internal usage, but
%		they can be retrieved or executed by wrapping them in 
%		\txtcmd{makeatletter} and \txtcmd{makeatother} (required due to the @
%		sign). Some of these are also meant to be sections of code you can
%		customize (see ``A note on customization'' below). If so, their
%		implementation code is shown in this docs for copy-pasting to your
%		customization file.
%	\item \LaTeX{3} variables are formatted as |\scope_@@_varname_vartype|,
%		e.g., the local boolean variable |\l_@@_embargo_bool| (g is used instead
%		for global). Control sequences are formatted as |\@@_csname| or 
%		|\_@@_csname| (for private control sequences). If you want to use 
%		L3 control sequences or variables in customized code, you need to 
%		replace '|@@|' with '|_ugentdocs|'!
% \end{itemize}   
%
% \section*{A note on fonts}
%
% The house style of Ghent University recommends two fonts:
% \vspace{-\parskip}
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Primary font: UGent Panno Text. ``This font does justice to the historical
%    values of Ghent University and is simultaneously modern, distinctive and
%    charismatic.''
%    \watchout The Medium and SemiLight variants are needed for the logos,
%    so they are an absolute requirement. If the Normal variant is installed,
%    it is used by default for all class files. You can download it 
%    \href{https://ugentbe.sharepoint.com/sites/intranet-communicatie/SitePages/en/corporate-design.aspx}
%    {from the intranet}.
% \item Secondary font: Arial. This is normally used for office templates
%    distributed outside of Ghent University. Since \LaTeX{} outputs PDFs,
%    this is not a concern. Still, you can override the font to Arial should
%    you wish (see Part~I Section~\ref{S:fonts}).
% \end{itemize}
% \vspace{-\parskip}
% As both official fonts are sans-serif styled, a similar
% math font is preferred. For this, we load the Noto Sans Math font (if it is
% installed), with a TeX Gyre DejaVu Math fallback.
%
%
% \section*{A note on compilers}
% These \watchout class files should be ready to use with all common modern
% \LaTeX{} compilers (XeLaTeX{}, \LuaLaTeX{}, \ldots) and \LaTeX{}2e kernel
% versions newer than October 2020 from the major \TeX{}-distributions
% (TeTeX, TexLive, MikTeX).
% Using an old \LaTeX{} + dvips setup or PDF\LaTeX{}, is
% not supported due to the logo implementation. So ditch the route via dvi
% and abandon the old PDF\LaTeX{} compiler.
% If you experience other problems, please inform the author.
%
% \section*{A note on customization}
% \label{S:customization}
% We do not recommend altering the UGent house style too much, and we 
% certainly do not recommend altering any of the class files directly.
% You can customize the classes by overwriting any of the commands in 
% Part~\ref{P:I} in a .sty file. A recommended naming scheme is
% |<classname>-customization.sty|, e.g. |ugentphd-customization.sty|.
% You can then load this customization using
% \txtcmd{usepackage\{ugentphd-customization\}}.
% Be sure to overwrite commands, e.g. use \txtcmd{renewcommand} and
% \txtcmd{RenewDocumentCommand}. Colours can be adjusted using e.g.:
% {\setlength{\topsep}{0pt}\setlength{\partopsep}{0pt}
% \begin{verbatim}
% \ExplSyntaxOn
% \str_if_eq:VnTF \l_@@_colormodel_tl {cmyk} {
%    \definecolor{<customcolor>}{cmyk}{<C,M,Y,K>} % From 0 to 1
% }{
%    \definecolor{<customcolor>}{RGB}{<R,G,B>} % From 0 to 255
% }
% \ExplSyntaxOff
% \colorlet{titlecolor}{<customcolor>}
% \end{verbatim}
% }
